NOTES = r"""
Paper grounding and Step 0.  Section 2 defines the generalized Sudoku exactly:
there are n^2 integer variables in 1..n, three column permutations pi_1,
pi_2,pi_3 of the block-diagonal comparison matrix A, componentwise nonzero
constraints A_pi_r x <> 0, and optional givens.  Lemmas 2 and 3 prove that a
block of A enforces precisely that its n entries are the symbols 1,...,n.
Section 3 identifies ordinary rows, columns, and square blocks as the three
partitions, while allowing arbitrary permutations gives gerechte designs.

The certificate-producing algorithm is explicit.  Theorem 5.1, extended to all
three partitions by Theorem 5.2, states

  x = (A_pi^T sgn(A_pi x) + (n+1)1)/2.

Equivalently, each value is one plus the number of smaller entries in its
comparison block.  Scanning all pair signs costs Theta(n^3).  This immediately
rules out Track A for the distribution exposed here.  Section 1 also names
brute force, pencil-and-paper methods, branch-and-cut, and Algorithm X; Section
3 notes successful exact-cover software.  Those are the easy-method warnings
that make an unqualified planted-Sudoku hardness claim untenable.

Track-B construction.  For prime n, cells have hidden coordinates (r,c) in
F_n^2.  The three paper partitions are r=constant, c=constant, and r+c=constant.
Choose v outside {0,1}, arbitrary w, nonzero a, and b outside {0,1,2,3}, and set

  x(r,c) = 1 + (a * inv(r + v*c + w) + b mod n),  inv(0)=0.

The inner affine form is a bijection along every row, column, and region, and
inversion and the outer affine map are permutations of F_n.  Thus every group
contains 1..n exactly once.  Independent random permutations hide cell IDs and
all three group labels.  The published comparison matrices are exactly
sgn(A_pi_1 x), with four redundant full comparison rows included only to make
the probe ranks writable without triangular-string arithmetic.  The generator
chooses the four coefficients first and never solves what it emits.

The compact route uses the two named row anchors.  Comparing their region rows
gives a single n-cycle T; its successive region labels are the hidden residues
0,1,...,n-1.  The origin row and column label every column and row group by
lookup.  Four named probe cells were inverse-generated to have ranks 1,2,3,4.
Their hidden column coordinates are 0,1,2,3, so equality of cross-ratios makes
the equation for v linear.  Two difference equations then recover w and b, and
one product recovers a.  At hard this needs at most 126 exact arithmetic
operations plus 264 sign reads, versus 296,274 core operations for full
Theorem-5.1 reconstruction.  This is no-tool compression, not a claim of
computational intractability.

Attack hardening.  Numeric cell and group IDs are independent random
permutations, defeating raw-ID and encounter-order coordinate guesses.  Fixed
small coefficients fail because all four coefficients are sampled uniformly in
their declared ranges.  Structure-aware random certificates sample exactly the
four legal coefficient ranges—there are no bogus coordinate permutations in
the denominator—and still produce no hits in 200,000 shipping samples.  The
successful theorem algorithm is reported separately, as Track B requires.

Canonicalization.  Named anchors fix origin and units.  canonical_key derives
the normalized coordinates and coefficients from public incidence and signs,
expresses probes and givens in those coordinates, and takes the lesser of the
row/column orientations.  It therefore ignores cell ordering, arbitrary cell
and group IDs, and transposition.  The G8 test composes these maps and carries
the symbolic witness through them.
""".strip()


